{#-
  methodology.html.j2: /methodology/ (Milestone 2 step 7). The method text comes from
  docs/ranking-methodology.md, cut out by tff_site.pages at build time, so the page and the
  document can't drift; the rest comes from the catalog (ranks, bands, tiers, source credits,
  licenses, this run). Context: every page's, plus `heading` and `method` (pages.py).

  Anchors are stable, whatever the document's headings say: the section ids below, and
  #source-<id> on each source's credit, which the details panel links to (site/CONTRACT.md
  section 8). tests/site/test_pages.py pins them. The page needs no script, so it loads none.
  Page text is a draft for the owner's approval (M2 step 7). Styles: site/css/40-pages.css.
-#}
{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{# The Project rank's label is "Used in projects" (site ruling 2026-09-25). #}
{% set survey_titles = {"desktop": "Desktop installs", "project": "Used in projects: websites, code and apps"} %}
{% set unranked_meaning = {
  "no_deliberate_evidence": "The font's only evidence in this rank is installs that Linux systems made on their own, and every rank except Desktop: most installed leaves those out. The font keeps a tag naming those systems or packages, and its place in Desktop: most installed.",
  "no_evidence": "None of this rank's sources counted the font.",
  "too_new": "The font has been on this rank's sources for less than 60 days.",
} %}
{% set state_meaning = {
  "observed": "the source counted the font.",
  "censored": "the source could have counted the font, but its count is below the source's floor or missing. It still counts, as a low value.",
  "not_covered": "the source doesn't carry the font, so it is left out rather than counted as zero.",
  "too_new": "the font has been on the source for less than 60 days, so it doesn't count there yet.",
} %}
{% block scripts %}{% endblock %}
{% block main %}
<article class="page page-method">
<h1>{{ heading }}</h1>
<p class="page-lead">We rank fonts by public counts of how often they are installed on computers and used in websites, code and apps. The method text on this page is taken from the <a href="{{ method.full_text_url }}">full methodology on GitHub</a> each time the site is built, so the two always agree.</p>
<p class="page-meta">Data from {{ method.run_date }}. Method version {{ method.method_version }}.{% if method.stale %} <a href="#this-run">{{ method.stale | length }} stale {{ "source" if method.stale | length == 1 else "sources" }}</a>.{% endif %}</p>

<nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-h">
<h2 id="toc-h" class="toc-title">On this page</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#in-plain-words">In plain words</a></li>
<li><a href="#desktop-views">The two desktop views</a></li>
<li><a href="#reading-ranks">Reading the ranks</a></li>
<li><a href="#tiers">Confidence tiers</a></li>
<li><a href="#biases">Known biases</a></li>
<li><a href="#sources">Sources and credits</a></li>
<li><a href="#licenses">Licenses</a></li>
<li><a href="#this-run">This run</a></li>
</ul>
</nav>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="in-plain-words">In plain words</h2>
{{ method.plain_words }}
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="desktop-views">The two desktop views</h2>
{{ method.desktop_views }}
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="reading-ranks">Reading the ranks</h2>
<h3 id="ranks">The ranks</h3>
<p>Choose a rank at the top of the list. Each one answers a different question:</p>
<dl class="terms">
{% for view in method.views %}
<dt>{{ view.label }}</dt>
<dd>{{ view.measures }}{% if not view.available %} Not shown yet.{% endif %}</dd>
{% endfor %}
</dl>
<p>For the most popular fonts of one kind, such as serif or handwriting, use the Category filter.</p>

<h3 id="scores">Scores</h3>
<p>Each ranked font shows a score from 0 to 100, and a blue bar that long. The score is the font's standing among all the fonts the sources track: a font scoring 90 stands above about 90% of them. It is not the chance that a given person chose the font.</p>
<p>The list is in score order. Filters only hide fonts, so a font's score is the same whatever else is shown.</p>
<p>A hollow bar marks a score that rests on one kind of source. The two-source rule keeps such a font out of the numbered top 100, and its score may move more from month to month. In Developers & apps every score rests on one kind of source, so a note above the list says so once instead.</p>
<p>A font's details keep its exact place in the top 100 of each rank. Past 100 the data can't tell fonts apart precisely, so the details give a band instead: {% for band in method.bands %}{{ band }}{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.</p>

<h3 id="not-ranked">Fonts that aren't ranked</h3>
<p>A font can be in the catalog but not ranked in one view. It is listed last, with the reason:</p>
<dl class="terms">
{% for item in method.unranked %}
<dt>Not ranked: {{ item.label }}</dt>
<dd>{{ unranked_meaning[item.key] }}</dd>
{% endfor %}
</dl>

<h3 id="evidence-states">Each source's view of a font</h3>
<p>A font's details show its rank in every source, or one of these states:</p>
<ul>
{% for item in method.states %}
<li><strong>{{ item.label | capitalize }}</strong>: {{ state_meaning[item.key] }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
<p>A Linux source can also be "left out of" some ranks: it counted the font, but Linux systems install that font on their own, and only Desktop: most installed counts such installs.</p>
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="tiers">Confidence tiers</h2>
<p>Every ranked font, numbered or in a band, comes with a tier that says how firm its place is. The details also show a range: the ranks the font would likely take if the sources or their weights changed a little.</p>
<dl class="terms">
{% for item in method.tiers %}
<dt>Tier {{ item.tier }}</dt>
<dd>{{ item.text }}</dd>
{% endfor %}
</dl>
<p>In the methodology's own words:</p>
<div class="quoted">
{{ method.confidence }}
</div>
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="biases">Known biases</h2>
{{ method.biases }}
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="sources">Sources and credits</h2>
<p>The ranks are built from counts that others publish. Thank you to all of them. The full methodology gives each source's weight and how its counts are cleaned.</p>
{% for survey in method.surveys %}
<h3 id="sources-{{ survey.key }}">{{ survey_titles[survey.key] }}</h3>
<ul class="sources">
{% for source in survey.sources %}
<li class="source" id="source-{{ source.id }}">
<h4 class="source-name">{{ source.name }}</h4>
<p>{{ source.measures }}</p>
<dl class="facts">
<dt>Data from</dt>
<dd>{{ source.data_date }}{% if source.stale %} <strong class="stale">Stale</strong>: its latest update failed, so its previous data is used, for up to 2 months.{% endif %}</dd>
<dt>License or terms</dt>
<dd>{{ source.license }}</dd>
<dt>Website</dt>
<dd><a href="{{ source.url }}">{{ source.link_label }}</a></dd>
</dl>
{% if not source.publish_rank %}
<p class="source-note">It counts toward the ranks, but a font's rank in this source isn't shown.</p>
{% endif %}
</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endfor %}
<p>The lists of candidate fonts and the license facts we collect come from other sources, credited in the <a href="{{ method.credits_url }}">project's README on GitHub</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="licenses">Licenses</h2>
<p>The ranking data is published under <a href="{{ method.data_license.url }}">{{ method.data_license.name }}</a>{% if not method.data_license.provisional %}, and that choice is final{% endif %}. You may share and adapt it, also commercially, if you credit Purely Free Fonts and share what you make from it under the same license. The repository says <a href="{{ method.data_license.scope_url }}">which files the data license covers</a>.</p>
<p>The code that collects the data and builds this site is under the <a href="{{ method.code_license.url }}">{{ method.code_license.name }}</a>.</p>
<p>The fonts are not ours. Each keeps its own license, linked from its details. The counts come from the <a href="#sources">sources above</a>, under their own terms.</p>
</section>

<section class="page-section">
<h2 id="this-run">This run</h2>
<dl class="facts">
<dt>Data from</dt>
<dd>{{ method.run_date }}</dd>
<dt>Method version</dt>
<dd>{{ method.method_version }}</dd>
<dt>Stale sources</dt>
{% if method.stale %}
<dd>
<ul>
{% for source in method.stale %}
<li><a href="#source-{{ source.id }}">{{ source.name }}</a>, data from {{ source.data_date }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
</dd>
{% else %}
<dd>None: every source updated this month.</dd>
{% endif %}
</dl>
<p>The data is refreshed every month. A source is stale when its update fails: its previous data is used for up to 2 months, then it is dropped until it works again.</p>
<p>Read the <a href="{{ method.full_text_url }}">full methodology on GitHub</a> for the formulas, weights, checks and decisions behind all of this.</p>
</section>
</article>
{% endblock %}
